      <section id="introduction">
        <h2>1. Introduction</h2>
        <p>Learned robot dynamics can cut the cost of a transition and provide derivatives of that transition with respect to state and action. Those are separate sources of potential speedup. Optimizing a policy through an inaccurate derivative can spend fewer imagined steps and still produce a worse controller on the original simulator.</p>
        <p>We test whether direct policy gradients reach useful Newton performance sooner than PPO on the same learned dynamics. We measure both the behavior of the learned step over open-loop horizons and the closed-loop performance of the resulting policies on Newton. The comparison isolates the policy update from changes to the simulator architecture.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="related">
        <h2>2. Related work</h2>
        <p>NeRD learns robot-specific state transitions and uses geometric contact information from an analytical simulator. FlashNeRD reduces forward cost with a streaming attention architecture and extends the interface to variable contacts and fixed-base two-way coupling. Its ANYmal learning comparison uses PPO across dynamics backends. <a href="#ref-nerd">[1]</a> <a href="#ref-flashnerd">[2]</a></p>
        <p>SuperMimic uses short imagined rollouts to backpropagate policy gradients through a differentiable world model and updates that model as policy data arrive. The present pilot tests the policy-gradient mechanism on a frozen model. Freezing makes it possible to hold the model and logged data constant for the policy comparison. <a href="#ref-supermimic">[3]</a></p>
        <p>NeurALL studies a related question for deformables. Its cable experiments optimize applied forces from observed material state and replay the controls in Newton. Controller performance for articulated bodies and target matching for cables require separate metrics and datasets. <a href="https://maxkra15.github.io/neurall/">NeurALL report.</a></p>
      </section>
