      <section id="reproduction">
        <h2>6. NeRD replication</h2>
        <p>The original NeRD checkpoint and the published FlashNeRD comparisons answer different questions. The release audit records the version, training teacher, state/action conventions and what ran. The FlashNeRD rows below are author-reported references.</p>
        {{ released_baseline }}
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>FlashNeRD paper Table I. CartPole prefix-averaged NRMSE (lower is better) and model-only throughput on a GB200. These columns describe the paper's own held-out trajectories, training normalization and replay timing. [2]</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Paper model</th><th scope="col" class="num">E<sub>1</sub></th><th scope="col" class="num">E<sub>15</sub></th><th scope="col" class="num">E<sub>100</sub></th><th scope="col" class="num">E<sub>200</sub></th><th scope="col" class="num">Steps/s at B=16,384</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Optimized NeRD</th><td class="num">5.89e−5</td><td class="num">4.78e−4</td><td class="num">8.43e−3</td><td class="num">0.106</td><td class="num">1.22M</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">FlashNeRD</th><td class="num">4.41e−5</td><td class="num">1.73e−4</td><td class="num">3.23e−3</td><td class="num">0.0599</td><td class="num">48.1M</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>FlashNeRD reports CartPole training on about 9,000 trajectories for five million updates. Its ANYmal experiment crosses a training-reward threshold at 34.3 s, compared with 93.2 s for Optimized NeRD and 129.9 s for MJWarp. Those times start with a ready dynamics model and count PPO iteration durations. Final ANYmal evaluation in MJWarp yields mean returns of 28.0, 27.6 and 27.8, respectively. <a href="#ref-flashnerd">[2, Sections IV-A and IV-B]</a></p>
        <p>A direct FlashNeRD result needs its weights or a validated reproduction of its training data, observation and contact processing, normalization, CUDA graph timing and policy environment. The CartPole pilot provides a controlled intervention on the policy optimizer. ANYmal velocity tracking and G1 contact-rich control remain separate replication targets.</p>
      </section>
